WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 206: The Persian Empire: Cyrus the Great, the Cyrus Cylinder, and the Return of the Exiles

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.480
[Rebecca] Today we enter the silver realm of the Medo-Persian Empire.

4
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:20.160
[Rebecca] Emerging from the Zagros Mountains, Cyrus the Great transformed world governance, instituting what many historians revere as the first charter of human rights.

5
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:28.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Cyrus the Great, the Cyrus Cylinder, and the return to Jerusalem.

6
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:41.360
[George] In the 6th century before Christ, the Persian tribes were small, pastoral vassals living in the rugged highlands of Anshan in southwestern Iran, subjugated by their northern kinsmen, the Medes.

7
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.720
[George] But in 559 BC, an extraordinary young prince named Cyrus I.

8
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.800
[George] ascended the Persian throne.

9
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.1000
[George] Combining brilliant military tactics with unmatched personal magnetism, Cyrus united the disparate Persian clans.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.080
[George] When the Median king Astyages marched to crush the upstart prince, Cyrus rallied his horsemen.

11
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:09.760
[George] In a stunning reversal, the Median army, alienated by Astygius's cruelty, mutinied and surrendered to Cyrus.

12
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:21.440
[George] Rather than sacking the Median capital of Ecbatana, Cyrus treated Astygius with honor, integrating Median generals into his high command and forging a dual Medo-Persian superpower.

13
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:34.480
[George] Cyrus then marched west into Asia Minor, defeating the fabulously wealthy King Croesus of Lydia at Sardis, subduing the Greek coastal cities of Ionia, and extending Persian dominion to the Aegean Sea.

14
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:47.760
[George] By 539 BC, Cyrus turned his gaze toward Babylon, conquering the Mesopotamian Empire with astonishing swiftness and positioning Persia as the undisputed colossus of the ancient Near East.

15
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.280
[George] Cyrus's early campaigns established a new standard of military chivalry in antiquity.

16
00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:05.400
[George] Unlike the Assyrian kings who mutilated and flayed conquered monarchs, Cyrus spared King Croesus of Lydia, appointing him as a senior counselor and preserving the cultural heritage of Sardis.

17
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:18.120
[George] Cyrus's tomb at Passargeti, a simple stepped limestone chamber inscribed with his modest epitaph, stood as a monument of personal restraint admired even by Alexander the Great two centuries later.

18
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:29.720
[George] When Cyrus entered Babylon in October 539 BC, the world expected the typical orgy of slaughter, looting, and enslavement perfected by Assyria and Babylon.

19
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:36.440
[George] Instead, Cyrus astonished the ancient world by enacting a revolutionary policy of imperial benevolence.

20
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:48.280
[George] In 1879, British museum archaeologists excavating Babylon discovered a nine-inch baked clay barrel covered in Akkadian cuneiform, the famous Cyrus cylinder.

21
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.920
[George] On this historic artifact, Cyrus declared his imperial philosophy.

22
00:02:52.920 --> 00:03:03.480
[George] When I entered Babylon as a friend and established the seat of government in the palace of the ruler amongst jubilation and rejoicing, my numerous troops strolled through Babylon in peace.

23
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.520
[George] I did not allow anyone to terrorize the land.

24
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.840
[George] I sought the welfare of the city of Babylon and all its sacred centers.

25
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:19.240
[George] I collected all their previous inhabitants and returned them to their habitations, reversing two centuries of Mesopotamian state terror.

26
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:34.200
[George] Cyrus permitted conquered nations to practice their ancestral religions, speak their native tongues, and rebuild their shattered shrines, creating an enduring model of decentralized federal empire that secured loyalty through gratitude rather than fear.

27
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:42.920
[George] The Cyrus Cylinder, often called the world's first Declaration of Human Rights, was buried in the foundations of the Esagilla Temple in Babylon.

28
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:54.240
[George] Its cuneiform text reveals an enlightened imperial strategy, fostering domestic peace and religious liberty across conquered territories to ensure lasting imperial stability.

29
00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:02.000
[George] In 1971, the United Nations celebrated the artifact as a timeless symbol of cultural and religious freedom.

30
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:16.960
[George] For the Jewish exiles living along the canals of Babylon, Cyrus's policy was nothing short of miraculous divine deliverance, fulfilling the exact prophecies uttered by Isaiah more than a century earlier, which had called Cyrus by name.

31
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:27.440
[George] And shall perform all my pleasure, even saying to Jerusalem, Thou shalt be built, and to the temple, thy foundation shall be laid.

32
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:36.640
[George] In 538 BC, the first year of his reign over Babylon, Cyrus issued a formal royal decree recorded in the Biblical Book of Ezra.

33
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:47.120
[George] Thus saith Cyrus King of Persia, the Lord God of heaven hath given me all the kingdoms of the earth, and he hath charged me to build him an house at Jerusalem, which is in Judah.

34
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.600
[George] Who is there among you of all his people?

35
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.200
[George] His God be with him, and let him go up to Jerusalem.

36
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.280
[George] And build the house of the Lord.

37
00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:08.960
[George] Cyrus did not merely permit the Jews to return, he dipped into the royal treasury to finance their travel, and ordered the return of 5,400 gold and silver vessels that Nebuchadnezzar had plundered from Solomon's temple.

38
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.360
[George] The royal proclamation of Cyrus sent shockwaves of joy through the Jewish communities of Mesopotamia.

39
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:29.280
[George] For the first time in ancient history, an imperial ruler actively funded the restoration of a conquered people's sacred temple from the royal treasury, granting the Jewish elders full authority to draw timber from imperial forests in Lebanon.

40
00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:47.880
[George] In the spring of 537 BC, a courageous pioneer caravan of 42,360 Jewish men, women, and children, accompanied by 7,000 servants, began the arduous four-month trek across 800 miles of scorched desert back to the ruins of Judah.

41
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:57.400
[George] Led by Prince Zarababel, a descendant of the royal line of David, and Jeshua the high priest, the returnees arrived to a scene of utter desolation.

42
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:03.240
[George] Jerusalem was a ghost town of charred stones, wild thornbushes, and collapsed towers.

43
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:16.040
[George] Undeterred, their first act was to clear the debris atop Mount Moriah and re erect the altar of burnt offerings, offering morning and evening sacrifices under the open autumn sky during the Feast of Tabernacles.

44
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:24.200
[George] In the second year of their return, with cedar timbers imported from Lebanon, masons laid the foundation stones of the second temple.

45
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:29.160
[George] The priests blew silver trumpets and cymbals, singing psalms of praise.

46
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:44.920
[George] Yet the biblical record notes a poignant contrast, while the young shouted for joy, the old men who had seen the towering glory of Solomon's temple wept with loud voices, seeing the humble scale of the new foundation, their cries mingling in the desert wind.

47
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:53.640
[George] The return journey from Babylon to Jerusalem across the desert was perilous, fraught with heat, thirst, and hostile nomadic raiders.

48
00:06:53.640 --> 00:07:05.720
[George] Yet the forty-two thousand pilgrims marched with singing, carrying five thousand sacred vessels of gold and silver back to Mount Moriah, sustained by daily prayers and sacred psalms of ascent.

49
00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:22.320
[George] Following the death of Cyrus on the northern frontier fighting nomadic Masagedti horsemen, and the brief reign of his son Cambyses who conquered Egypt, the Persian Empire was reorganized by a brilliant administrative genius, Darius I, known as Darius the Great.

50
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:44.480
[George] Ascending the throne in 522 BC after putting down a dozen rebellions commemorated on the colossal Behistun rock inscription, Darius divided the vast empire into twenty administrative provinces called satrapies, each governed by a royal satrap, a military commander, and a state secretary who operated as the king's eyes and ears.

51
00:07:44.480 --> 00:08:05.600
[George] Darius standardized taxation, introduced the gold Derek coin that became the universal currency of Mediterranean commerce, and created an elite 10,000 man infantry bodyguard called the Immortals, so named because whenever a soldier fell in battle, another immediately stepped into his place, keeping the unit perpetually invincible.

52
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:14.880
[George] Under Darius, the Persian Empire spanned two million square miles, from the Indus River in India to the plains of Thrace in southeastern Europe.

53
00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:21.680
[George] Darius the Great's administrative reorganization turned Persia into the most efficiently governed empire of antiquity.

54
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:35.120
[George] His standard gold derrick coins and silver siglae established a predictable economic foundation that fueled international commerce from India to the Aegean Sea, while royal auditors managed taxation records in Susa.

55
00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:43.600
[George] To bind this colossal empire together, Darius engineered the ancient world's greatest infrastructure marvel, the royal road.

56
00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:58.800
[George] Stretching 1677 miles from the Aegean port of Sardis through the heart of Anatolia, crossing the Euphrates and Tigris to the imperial capital at Susa, the highway was paved and guarded by 111 post stations.

57
00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:08.920
[George] Mounted royal couriers on fresh horses relayed imperial dispatches across the entire length of the road in just seven days, a journey that took three months on foot.

58
00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.480
[George] Greek historian Herodotus famously marveled at their unyielding speed.

59
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:25.240
[George] Neither snow nor rain nor heat nor gloom of night stays these couriers from the swift completion of their appointed rounds, words immortalized today on the facade of New York City's central post office.

60
00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:33.000
[George] In the Persian homeland, Darius founded Persepolis, the ceremonial capital built upon a massive stone terrace.

61
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:45.480
[George] Adorned with the breathtaking Apadana Palace Hall, supported by seventy-foot fluted columns and carved with intricate stone reliefs depicting delegations from twenty-three nations bearing gifts in peaceful harmony.

62
00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.840
[George] The royal road was celebrated by Greek travelers as an unprecedented civil marvel.

63
00:09:50.840 --> 00:10:04.680
[George] With fresh horses waiting at every station, imperial couriers carried royal decrees across rugged mountain ranges and deserts in seven days, binding distant provinces to the court at Susa with remarkable speed and security.

64
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:15.400
[George] Back in Jerusalem, temple construction had stalled for sixteen years due to hostile opposition from neighboring Samaritans who bribed Persian court officials to issue halt orders.

65
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:21.720
[George] But under the impassioned preaching of the prophets Haggie and Zechariah, Zarababel resumed construction.

66
00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:30.600
[George] When the Persian governor of the Transeuphrates region, Tatanai, challenged their authority, the elders appealed to the original edict of Cyrus.

67
00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.960
[George] Darius the Great ordered a search of the royal archives.

68
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:39.320
[George] In the fortress of Ecpatana, the original parchment roll of Cyrus was discovered.

69
00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.040
[George] Darius issued a stern royal decree.

70
00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:55.360
[George] Not only was Tatanai ordered not to hinder the work, but he was commanded to provide royal tax revenues to pay for construction, warning that anyone who altered the decree would have a beam pulled from his house and be impaled upon it.

71
00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:11.760
[George] In 516 BC, exactly 70 years after Solomon's temple had been burned, the second temple of Jerusalem was completed and joyfully dedicated, standing as an enduring testament to the enlightened decree of Cyrus and the unyielding resilience of faith.

72
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:19.680
[George] The completion of the Second Temple in 516 BC marked the exact fulfillment of Jeremiah's 70-year prophecy.

73
00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:32.640
[George] Though modest compared to Solomon's golden grandeur, the Second Temple stood as an enduring monument to divine faithfulness, dedicated with joyous Passover celebrations under Persian imperial protection.

74
00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:41.200
[Rebecca] Under the tolerant banner of Persia, the Second Temple rose in Jerusalem, anchoring Jewish identity for centuries to come.

75
00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:50.320
[Rebecca] But within the marble halls of the royal winter palace at Susa, a mortal peril was gathering that threatened the total extinction of the Jewish people.

76
00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:58.320
[Rebecca] Next week we continue our Persian saga with episode 207, Queen Esther, Xerxes and the Deliverance of Purim.

77
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:07.840
[Rebecca] The palace beauty pageant, the arrogant pride of Haman, Mordecai's Defiant Stand, the Queen Who Risked Death to approach the Golden Scepter, and the Feast of Purim.

78
00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:12.080
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

